{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw1fYRxwKEV6jWazx6NY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CWXgmUUg7hZPMiGKMnYEkU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'; t=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b,cnt=1):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(a)>=1, a[:60]\n    t=t.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"the extrapolation gap is the clearest measured result.\",\"the extrapolation gap is the largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an interval.\")\nrep(\"The dominant, statistically unambiguous finding is a large gap between regimes:\",\"The largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an interval, is a gap between regimes:\")\nrep(\"The same pattern appears on the five development benchmarks.\",\"The same pattern appears on the five development benchmarks. Part of this gap may reflect the narrower score range of strong targets rather than extrapolation as such, which we did not test.\")\nrep(\"The clearest result is that targets stronger\",\"The largest measured difference is that targets stronger\")\nrep(\"(MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956), on both development and held-out benchmark groups, and the design\",\"(MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956), on both development and held-out benchmark groups (point estimates, without intervals, and possibly partly due to the narrower score range of strong targets), and the design\")\n# Zhang eleven\nrep(\"A comparison of eleven benchmark-prediction methods across many benchmarks is reported by Zhang et al.\\\\ \\\\citep{Zhang2025HowBP}\",\"A comparison of several benchmark-prediction methods across many benchmarks is reported by Zhang et al.\\\\ \\\\citep{Zhang2025HowBP}\")\n# baselines ignore regime label -> qualified\nrep(\"The regime label is supplied to the estimator with the source data; baselines ignore it.\",\"The regime label is supplied to the candidate estimator with the source data; the baseline functions in the evaluator take only the source matrix and the target responses on their chosen items (\\\\texttt{source\\\\_evidence/evaluate\\\\_final.py}, baseline functions).\")\n# design details provenance\nrep(\"\\\\subsection{Selected estimator}\\n\",\"\\\\subsection{Selected estimator}\\nImplementation details below are taken from the readable source of the evaluated configuration (\\\\texttt{source\\\\_evidence/candidate\\\\_program.py}; the extension of Table~\\\\ref{tab:search} is in \\\\texttt{source\\\\_evidence/extension\\\\_program.py}).\\n\")\n# qualification\nold=t[t.index(\"The final evaluation is labeled \\\\emph{qualified} by the evaluator;\"):t.index(\"\\\\section{Results}\")]\nnew=\"The final evaluation is labeled \\\\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement about the evaluation outcome only, not as evidence of superiority or generalization. The evidence files mark the candidate-side diagnostics as unverified and provide no gate-level diagnostics, so we do not state which criteria underlie the label and attribute nothing to any individual criterion.\\n\\n\"\nt=t.replace(old,new)\nrep(\"Predictions therefore depend on the target's revealed answers, as required of a valid estimator. The controls establish protocol validity, not accuracy; they were evaluated on a single benchmark and seed.\",\"Predictions therefore depend on the target's revealed answers, as one would require of a valid estimator. The controls do not measure accuracy. Each control is a single episode matched to one real random-target episode, on one benchmark (MMLU-Pro) and the first seed of the final evaluation (\\\\texttt{source\\\\_evidence/evaluate\\\\_final.py}, episode plan), so the control deltas carry no benchmark or seed variance.\")\nrep(\"The ``qualified'' label of the evaluation concerns protocol validity only.\",\"The evaluator's ``qualified'' label is not a superiority or generalization claim, and no gate-level diagnostics are available to interpret it further.\")\nrep(\"passes protocol-validity checks and negative controls,\",\"passes both negative controls (each a single episode),\")\nrep(\"it also passes both negative controls.\",\"it also passes both negative controls (single episodes).\")\nrep(\"The ``IRT-style baseline is a fixed-configuration\",\"The ``IRT-style baseline is a fixed-configuration\") if False else None\nopen(p,'w').write(t)\nE\ngrep -n \"qualified\\|protocol validity\\|protocol-validity\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2995,"cache_read_input_tokens":89530,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2995},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55f4037c-dcdf-4df0-8ede-0755764998f5","uuid":"16ff4346-568f-4d79-a12b-aa6d374c6a3b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:50:53.509Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw1fXZt85woX2eM32VB4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CWXgmUUg7hZPMiGKMnYEkU","type":"tool_result","content":"107:\\paragraph{Controls and qualification.} Two negative controls replace the target responses on the selected items: a permutation that assigns each target another target's answers, and a constant response pattern equal to the source majority. The final evaluation is labeled \\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement about the evaluation outcome only, not as evidence of superiority or generalization. The evidence files mark the candidate-side diagnostics as unverified and provide no gate-level diagnostics, so we do not state which criteria underlie the label and attribute nothing to any individual criterion.\n196:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The stacked configuration has lower point-estimate MAE than three matched baselines on five held-out benchmarks, passes both negative controls (each a single episode), and shows a large degradation for stronger-than-source targets. It does not establish a statistically resolved improvement over a random-subset mean, a superiority claim, or generalization to other benchmarks, budgets or score types. The evaluator's ``qualified'' label is not a superiority or generalization claim, and no gate-level diagnostics are available to interpret it further.\n266:Recorded wall-clock time per seeded run was 150.8, 77.4 and 75.1 seconds respectively for the three seeds. The evaluator reports 128 candidate calls observed over 32 episodes. Utility differences against anchor points and IRT-style baselines are not accompanied by intervals in the retained outputs. The evaluation was declared \\emph{qualified} with completed status; candidate-side diagnostics are recorded as unverified.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55f4037c-dcdf-4df0-8ede-0755764998f5","uuid":"5cac98cb-c973-4cd4-a37d-a4a17f8e11ed","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:50:53.577Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"107:\\paragraph{Controls and qualification.} Two negative controls replace the target responses on the selected items: a permutation that assigns each target another target's answers, and a constant response pattern equal to the source majority. The final evaluation is labeled \\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement about the evaluation outcome only, not as evidence of superiority or generalization. The evidence files mark the candidate-side diagnostics as unverified and provide no gate-level diagnostics, so we do not state which criteria underlie the label and attribute nothing to any individual criterion.\n196:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The stacked configuration has lower point-estimate MAE than three matched baselines on five held-out benchmarks, passes both negative controls (each a single episode), and shows a large degradation for stronger-than-source targets. It does not establish a statistically resolved improvement over a random-subset mean, a superiority claim, or generalization to other benchmarks, budgets or score types. The evaluator's ``qualified'' label is not a superiority or generalization claim, and no gate-level diagnostics are available to interpret it further.\n266:Recorded wall-clock time per seeded run was 150.8, 77.4 and 75.1 seconds respectively for the three seeds. The evaluator reports 128 candidate calls observed over 32 episodes. Utility differences against anchor points and IRT-style baselines are not accompanied by intervals in the retained outputs. The evaluation was declared \\emph{qualified} with completed status; candidate-side diagnostics are recorded as unverified.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
